# SDL3, and the two doors that were shut this morning.
#
#     heroes run examples/sdl/main.hero
#
# **This program is the acceptance test for panel 055's allow-list.** Until
# panel 055 SDL could not be bound at all, and the shape of the failure is worth
# keeping: it was unreachable through *both* clauses, and each diagnostic sent the
# author to the other one.
#
#     extern "SDL3/SDL.h" package "sdl3"
#       → error[ffi_package]: … answered with `-D_THREAD_SAFE` / `-Wl,-rpath,…`,
#         which this compiler does not pass on
#     extern "SDL3/SDL.h" link "SDL3"
#       → error[ffi_missing_header]: not on this machine's include path
#
# There was no third door. Panel 050 had cited Go's allow-list as the precedent
# for having one at all — Go shipped the idea without a filter and it became
# CVE-2018-6574 — and then adopted a list **narrower than Go's**, which documents
# `-D`, `-U`, `-I` and `-l`. Measured on one machine: **28 of 285 `.pc` files
# answer with a flag the narrow list rejected**, among them `ncurses`, `readline`,
# `x264` and all twelve of Qt6.
#
# **It is SDL3 and not SDL2, and that is not a detail.** The first version of this
# file bound SDL2, and SDL2 on a machine with Homebrew is two traps at once.
# `pkg-config --libs sdl2` answers `-lSDL2main`, and **`SDL2main` replaces the
# program's `main`** with SDL's own, which starts Cocoa — so every run of this
# corpus became a windowed application, eleven programs in three configurations,
# filling the author's dock with icons. And that machine's `libSDL2` is
# `sdl2-compat`, a shim that loads SDL3 at run time and, failing, opens a **modal
# dialog** and waits for a click. On an unattended run that is not a failure, it
# is a **hang**: two processes at 0% CPU for ninety minutes.
#
# SDL3 has neither. There is no `SDL3main` — the library was dropped — and no
# shim underneath. `docs/work/DECIDE.md` carries what the episode found, which
# is that this allow-list's property test asks what a flag does at **build** time
# and both of those things happen at **run** time.
#
# **It opens no window**, for the reason `examples/raylib/main.hero` learned first:
# a corpus program runs unattended on a headless runner, so the binding it
# exercises must need no screen. `SDL_GetRevision` is SDL's own build string and
# touches no display; `SDL_GetNumVideoDrivers` asks how many ways to draw exist,
# which is a question and not an attempt.
#
# What it does not prove: SDL's interesting surface is `SDL_Rect`, `SDL_Event` and
# `SDL_Surface`, all structs by value. That is `M-struct-passing`, which is the
# next milestone precisely because a third of every C boundary is behind it.

extern "SDL3/SDL.h" package "sdl3"
    function SDL_GetRevision() -> cstr
    function SDL_GetNumVideoDrivers() -> i32

function main()
    # The revision string is SDL's own, so its *content* is the library's business
    # and not this program's. What this asserts is that a `cstr` came back across
    # the boundary and became a `str` on this side.
    print("sdl3 named its build: ", len(validated(c: SDL_GetRevision()).default("")) > 0)

    # The count is a property of the machine, so the program prints whether there
    # is a way to draw rather than how many — which is the same on a laptop and on
    # a runner with no display.
    print("sdl3 knows a way to draw: ", SDL_GetNumVideoDrivers() > 0)

test "the binding reaches SDL3 and comes back"
    assert len(validated(c: SDL_GetRevision()).default("")) > 0
    assert SDL_GetNumVideoDrivers() >= 0
